  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Masters Vault",
    "overview": "IMSS Records keeps its catalog and its money in one dark, secure room. The dashboard should feel like that vault: a near-black field with a faint warm-and-cool glow at the edges, quiet panels laid on top, and figures that read as exact. Nothing in it is loud. The two lights in the room, Tape Amber and Console Teal, each do one job: amber marks money owed and the place you are, teal marks money earned and things confirmed.\n\nIt's a working tool used daily by label staff, and by artists checking what they're owed, often on a phone. Density is moderate. Tables carry the real information and sit in a full hairline grid. Money is always set in mono with tabular numerals, so columns line up and amounts can't be misread. Prose stays short and plain.\n\nThe same world serves both doors. The team side and the artist portal share the canvas, the panels, the sidebar and the type; the portal simply leads with the artist's balance and their releases.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Near-black vault ground with a diagonal amber → black → green → violet glow and soft blurred colour blobs behind everything.",
      "Translucent dark panels with 1px hairline borders and generous 14px corners.",
      "Two accents with fixed meanings: amber = owed / active / primary action, teal = earned / confirmed.",
      "Every amount in IBM Plex Mono with tabular figures.",
      "Sidebar navigation on desktop that collapses to a swipeable strip on phones."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Two Lights Rule",
        "body": "Amber means owed, active or act-now; teal means earned or confirmed. Never swap them, and never use either as decoration.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Readable Ink Rule",
        "body": "Text a person must read is Ivory Ink or Ash Ink. Smoke Ink is for icons and placeholders, never for words that carry information.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Statement Figures Rule",
        "body": "Every amount of money is set in IBM Plex Mono with tabular numerals and right-aligned in its column. Whole dollars on team overviews; cents wherever an artist reads a breakdown.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Scroll Rule",
        "body": "Only the content pane scrolls. Never add a second scroll container inside a page; wide tables scroll sideways inside their own bordered box.",
        "section": "layout"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Soft Lift Rule",
        "body": "Shadows are always offset and blurred, and only ever lift by 1px. No hard-edged block shadows, no coloured glows.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** set every amount in IBM Plex Mono with tabular numerals, right-aligned.",
      "**Do** keep amber for owed / active / primary and teal for earned / confirmed (The Two Lights Rule).",
      "**Do** use 14px corners on surfaces and buttons and 10px on inputs, with 1px Hairline borders.",
      "**Do** put tables in a full hairline grid with a shaded header row.",
      "**Do** show the arithmetic whenever you show an artist a total: gross, Inbound Banking Charge, label share, then their earnings.",
      "**Do** call the swift fee \"Inbound Banking Charge\" everywhere a person can read it.",
      "**Do** put responsive changes in the global stylesheet's media queries."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** use Smoke Ink (#686E6E) for text people need to read; it fails contrast on the vault ground.",
      "**Don't** add a second scrolling area inside a page.",
      "**Don't** use hard offset shadows, coloured glows or gradient text.",
      "**Don't** use Tailwind responsive prefixes (`sm:`, `md:`, `lg:`); breakpoints live in the stylesheet.",
      "**Don't** show an artist gross figures without the breakdown beside them.",
      "**Don't** open a modal for a delete; confirm inline."
    ]
  }
